            <ArticleTitle>Greetings from the endoplasmic reticulum (ER): Escaping ER thiol isomerases regulate thrombosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/jth.13916</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The unfolded protein response (UPR) is the primary cellular mechanism to ensure proteostasis, that is, the correct regulation of protein biosynthesis, folding, trafficking and degradation. The UPR is triggered by the accumulation of unfolded or misfolded proteins within the endopasmic reticulum (ER), and is characterized by the induction of chaperones, degradation of incorrectly folded proteins, and halting protein translation. Major chaperones activated by the UPR include calreticulin, certain heat shock proteins (HSP) such as the glucose regulated proteins GRP78 and GRP79, and the thiol isomerases. This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
